        brief: 'Fast and multidomain: smell, sinonasal symptoms, polyp score and lung function all moved within a month.',
        detailed: `Priessnitz §3.2.2–3.2.3 and Table 1: SNOT-22 improved by 34 points at 3 months; UPSIT showed 73% regained normal or near-normal smell; anosmia prevalence fell from 16/22 to 4/22 after 1 month; NPS regressed after 1 month; FEV1 rose 12.6% predicted after 1 month and stayed up at 3 months; ACQ-6 fell 1.3 points (baseline 1.6).\n\nApplication: a multicentre retrospective study found fewer exacerbations and median OCS bursts falling from 2 to 0 per year, regardless of baseline blood eosinophils (Wangberg 2022).\n\nCaveat: this is an uncontrolled before-after cohort with no placebo, so its 34-point SNOT-22 change is not comparable with the placebo-subtracted differences from RCTs (item q35). The draft's "SNOT-22 −24.35 to −34" range mixes the two.`,
