        brief: 'Eyes, eosinophils, joints. Conjunctivitis 15.8% vs 8.4% and keratitis 1.3% vs 0.2% (placebo) are class signals, highest in atopic dermatitis.',
        detailed: `Priessnitz §3.2.5: injection-site reactions, nasopharyngitis and transient eosinophilia; a subset, particularly those with atopic dermatitis, develop conjunctivitis or keratitis at the quoted rates; in CRSwNP and AERD studies epistaxis, headache and arthralgia occur occasionally at rates comparable with placebo, mostly mild. Arthralgia is described in an AERD case series (Priessnitz ref 59).\n\nCaveat: the ocular rates come from atopic dermatitis trials; ocular events are less frequent in CRSwNP trials, so quoting 15.8% to a CRSwNP patient overstates the risk.\n\nKnowledge addition: the transient eosinophilia matters when oral steroids are tapered, because an underlying eosinophilic vasculitis (EGPA) can surface.\n\nPearl: the distractors belong to zileuton, benralizumab and reslizumab.`,
